Changes with the nginx-cache-turbo-module (unreleased)

    *) Bugfix: response headers generated by downstream nginx output filters,
       including the documented `add_header X-Cache-Turbo
       $cache_turbo_status always`, are no longer captured as origin metadata.
       Previously a cache HIT could replay the stored MISS value beside the
       freshly evaluated HIT value. Cache blobs now preserve a pre-filter
       snapshot of upstream headers and derive freshness only from that
       snapshot.

    *) Bugfix: admin warm `url_file` reads now require nginx `--with-threads`
       and an available/default thread pool, and the file contents are read via
       nginx's thread pool instead of a synchronous worker read. Missing thread
       support or pool setup returns a diagnosable 500 JSON response and logs
       the missing prerequisite.

    *) Compatibility note (since v1.1.0): the shipped circuit-breaker defaults
       are on, 5 failures in 10s, and a 30s open window; the shipped
       keep-stale default is 24h. A dead or flapping origin can therefore
       produce local 503 responses once the shared zone trips. Use
       `cache_turbo_breaker off;` and/or `cache_turbo_keep_stale off;` to opt
       out of those two defaults.

    *) Bugfix: an auto-Vary marker-store failure could unconditionally purge
       the variant key after publishing its L1 body. If another request
       replaced that variant during the marker write, the first request's
       rollback deleted the replacement. Rollback now pins and conditionally
       deletes only the exact blob written by the failing request; isolated
       failures still remove their unindexed variant.

    *) Bugfix: OpenSSL digest update failures are now sticky, and digest
       failures from auto-Vary key derivation and the admin single-key purge
       are propagated instead of letting callers hash or act on invalid output.
       Successful SHA-256 bytes, cache keys, marker keys, and variant-index
       names are unchanged.

    *) Bugfix: an L1 all-purge could run forever while other workers refilled
       the zone between its 512-entry mutex batches. The walk now consumes a
       finite start-of-call entry budget. If concurrent refill still leaves
       entries at the final lock-held observation, the admin endpoint returns
       500 with {"purged":N,"l1":"incomplete"}, while still completing a
       configured Redis L2 purge; if both tiers are incomplete their fields are
       combined in the response. An idle purge still drains both LRU queues
       completely and returns 200.

    *) Security: admin-triggered warm subrequests now rebuild their request
       headers from a Host/User-Agent allowlist before contacting the origin.
       Previously they inherited every header from the admin POST, so
       Authorization, Proxy-Authorization, cookies, and application-specific
       admin tokens could reach the warmed origin. Background-refresh
       validators are still added explicitly after the rebuild. Affects: all
       versions through 1.1.0. Mitigation without upgrading: invoke the warm
       endpoint without credentials or sensitive custom headers.

    *) Bugfix: a malformed valued request Cache-Control max-stale directive,
       such as max-stale=abc or max-stale=, was treated as the valid bare form
       and granted unlimited stale tolerance. Invalid valued forms now grant no
       stale tolerance; bare max-stale and valid numeric bounds are unchanged.
       Repeated request bounds in one combined field now merge restrictively,
       matching split field-lines, while commas and directive-looking text in
       quoted extension values remain inert.

    *) Feature: the admin JSON endpoint now reports "lock_ttl", the effective
       single-flight lock TTL in seconds for the admin location -- after
       preset-band resolution, inheritance, and the parse-time clamp to the
       time_t ceiling (4294967295). It is the object's only effective-config
       field; every other key remains a zone counter or gauge. This makes an
       oversized cache_turbo_lock_ttl observable: the directive clamps rather
       than rejects, and until now nothing could distinguish a clamped value
       from the one the operator wrote. The Prometheus rendering is unchanged.
       ci/tools/lint-admin-buffer-budget.py now measures the JSON reply's
       buffer budget as well as the Prometheus one, so a field added to the
       format string but not to the sizeof() skeleton fails the build instead
       of silently truncating the last field.

    *) Performance: the L1 shared-memory rbtree ordered nodes on the caller's
       32-bit crc32 short hash instead of the full 64-bit key, so two keys
       colliding in the low 32 bits fell back to the memcmp identity tiebreak
       on every descent instead of separating on the primary compare. The
       rbtree key is now derived from a fixed-byte-order load of the first 8
       bytes of the existing 32-byte key hash; the memcmp tiebreak is
       unchanged, it is just reached less often. Measured with
       ci/tools/allocbench.sh at 2M keys: +6.4% rps, p99 482us -> 440us.

    *) Performance: access_l1() now skips vary_apply()'s lock-held auto-Vary
       marker lookup whenever a zone-wide marker-presence counter reads zero,
       instead of probing L1 on every hit regardless of whether the zone holds
       any markers. vary_apply() self-time went from 1.12-1.35% of profile
       samples to absent from the profile; that removed probe is the win.
       End-to-end throughput on the VARY=1 shape moved -0.55%, within
       run-to-run noise.

    *) Performance: blobref.refs, the zero-copy HIT reference count, is now
       ngx_atomic_t, and blob_release() decrements it without holding the zone
       mutex, taking the mutex only on the terminal 1->0 transition that frees
       the slab. Previously every completed HIT re-took the mutex purely to
       decrement a refcount, and at WORKERS=16 that made ngx_shmtx_lock the
       hottest symbol in the L1 hit-path profile. Measured with
       ci/tools/perf-profile.sh: ngx_shmtx self-time WORKERS=8 10.29% -> 4.50%,
       WORKERS=16 50.53% -> 10.95%; ngx_shmtx_lock's own share at WORKERS=16
       18.23% -> 4.07%.

    *) Performance: each worker process now fetches its SHA-256 EVP_MD once,
       via EVP_MD_fetch(), and reuses it for every cache-key digest, instead of
       handing OpenSSL 3.x's legacy EVP_sha256() handle to EVP_DigestInit_ex(),
       which implicitly re-fetched the provider -- a property-string parse plus
       hashtable lookup -- on every call. Falls back to EVP_sha256() on pre-3.0
       OpenSSL, LibreSSL, or a failed fetch. Measured with
       ci/tools/perf-profile.sh: EVP_DigestInit_ex self-time 5.55% -> 0.03%,
       and ossl_fnv1a_hash no longer appears in the profile.

    *) Security: cache_turbo_lock_ttl accepted an arbitrary, unbounded time
       value. The single-flight refresh path multiplies it by the live load
       factor and adds the result to the current time (now + lock_ttl) to
       compute refresh_lock_until -- an additive time_t overflow if an
       operator (or a templated config driven by an external value) supplied
       a large-enough lock_ttl. cache_turbo_lock_ttl is now clamped to
       NGX_HTTP_CACHE_TURBO_TTL_MAX at parse time, the same ceiling and
       clamp-not-reject contract cache_turbo_keep_stale already uses.
       Affects: all versions through 1.1.0.
       Exposure: config-write access only; no request can influence
       lock_ttl. Mitigation without upgrading: keep cache_turbo_lock_ttl
       within a sane range (seconds to low hours) in every location.

    *) Bugfix: cache_turbo_lock_ttl 0 was accepted by nginx -t and then
       silently coerced to 5s by two independent runtime self-heal sites in
       the single-flight refresh path, so the running config quietly meant
       something other than what the operator wrote. cache_turbo_lock_ttl 0
       is now rejected at parse time with a diagnostic pointing at
       "cache_turbo_lock off", the directive that actually disables
       single-flight locking. The runtime coercions themselves are left in
       place as defence in depth.

    *) Bugfix: cache_turbo_min_uses_window accepted any value
       ngx_conf_set_sec_slot would parse, with no range check, even though
       the field's own comment documents it as bounded by
       NGX_HTTP_CACHE_TURBO_MIN_USES_WINDOW_MAX (86400, one day) --
       an invariant nothing in the module enforced. The directive now has a
       hand-rolled setter that range-checks against
       NGX_HTTP_CACHE_TURBO_MIN_USES_WINDOW_MIN/_MAX (1..86400 seconds),
       matching cache_turbo_l2_negative_ttl's contract: 0 remains accepted
       and means OFF (today's default, unchanged).

    *) Security: cache_turbo_redis accepted an empty tls_ca= or tls_name=
       value. An empty tls_ca= silently widens an intentional certificate
       pin to the system CA store, since ngx_ssl_trusted_certificate() is
       only invoked when the configured CA path is non-empty; an empty
       tls_name= silently falls back to verifying against the DSN host
       instead of an operator-pinned name, at both the SNI-send and
       post-handshake-verify sites. Both are now rejected at config-parse
       time, the same treatment an empty L2 key prefix= already receives.
       Affects: all versions through 1.1.0, only where cache_turbo_redis
       tls=on is configured with tls_ca= or tls_name= present but blank.
       Exposure: config-write access only; the result of the prior behaviour
       was a silently weaker TLS trust configuration than the operator
       intended, not a request-reachable bypass. Mitigation without
       upgrading: audit any cache_turbo_redis line for tls_ca= or tls_name=
       with nothing after the "=".

    *) Security: an admin warm list supplied as a CRLF-terminated file
       (POST /_cache?url_file=...) could place a bare CR into the upstream
       request line. The "\r" trim moved the decoded URI's length but not the
       end of the RAW span, and it is the raw span -- still percent-escaped --
       that becomes the warm subrequest's unparsed_uri and is written to the
       origin verbatim. The safety check ran only on the decoded URI, so the
       bytes actually sent were never validated at all. The raw span is now
       trimmed with its own cursor (the list parser's own advance cursor must
       not move) and screened for control bytes before use.
       Affects: all versions through 1.1.0, on the admin endpoint only.
       Exposure: reaching it requires access to the admin endpoint, which is
       expected to be restricted; the result is a malformed origin request
       line, upstream-dependent 400s or desync on a lenient origin. No fix is
       available by configuration -- restrict the admin endpoint, or upgrade.

    *) Security: a hostile, compromised or MITM'd L2 (Redis) peer could turn a
       ~12-byte reply into a 16MB allocation, once per SCAN page, by declaring
       an element count the reply does not contain. The SMEMBERS and SCAN
       parsers sized their array from the declared count -- bounded only by
       the 1M element ceiling -- before validating a single element, giving
       roughly 1.4M:1 memory amplification. Both parsers now require the
       declared count to be backed by bytes actually on the wire (the
       shortest element being "$0\r\n", four bytes) before allocating.
       Affects: all versions through 1.1.0, only where an L2 backend is
       configured. Exposure: denial of service against the worker; the memory
       is pool-freed at request teardown, and no corruption is possible.
       Mitigation without upgrading: ensure the L2 peer is trusted and the
       link to it is authenticated.

    *) Security: a second cache_turbo_redis (or cache_turbo_memcached) in the
       same block was silently accepted and partially merged into the first
       instead of being rejected. The setter only overwrites what the new DSN
       actually carries -- userinfo, db and the TLS flag are written only when
       present -- so the first directive's username, password and database
       survived under the second directive's host, and a rediss:// first line
       could not be downgraded by a redis:// second one because redis_tls is
       never cleared on any path. An operator editing a config by adding a
       replacement line rather than changing the existing one therefore sent
       the original credentials to the new host, with no diagnostic. Both
       directives now reject a duplicate, as every other single-value
       directive already did.
       Affects: all versions through 1.1.0, only where an L2 backend is
       configured. Exposure: requires the ability to write the nginx config;
       the result is credentials disclosed to whichever host the second line
       names, and a TLS requirement that cannot be turned off once set.
       Mitigation without upgrading: ensure each block declares its L2
       backend exactly once, and check nginx -t output against the config as
       written rather than as intended.

    *) Bugfix: the variant-index self-heal bit (varidx_pending) was never
       initialised on a newly allocated cache node. Shared-memory slab
       allocations are not zeroed, so a node landing on a recycled slot could
       be born with the bit set; the first lookup then read it as a
       variant-index write that had been dropped and re-issued a Redis SADD
       for a key that never had one. This inflated the varidx_reissues
       counter past varidx_drops and produced spurious L2 writes on
       cold-miss traffic. The bit is now cleared in the same funnel that
       already clears the neighbouring second-chance bit.

    *) Bugfix: the cache_turbo_zone "admission=on|off" policy was ignored on
       reload. The only assignment to the shared-memory copy of the policy
       ran on the fresh-zone path, after both zone-inheritance paths had
       already returned, so a changed value took effect only on a full
       restart -- and silently, since nginx reported the reload as
       successful. The neighbouring protected_pct setting is re-read per
       request and did follow a reload, so an operator changing both saw one
       apply and had no indication the other had not.

    *) Bugfix: a background warm subrequest -- the stale-while-revalidate
       refresh and the new cache_turbo_store_head HEAD warm -- hit undefined
       behaviour in nginx's proxy module when the location's proxy_pass
       carried no URI component ("proxy_pass http://backend;", with no
       trailing slash and no path).
       nginx copies valid_unparsed_uri onto a subrequest only for cloned
       subrequests, so the warm took a different branch of
       ngx_http_proxy_create_request() than the client request it was spawned
       from -- one whose copy of the location's empty URI is guarded by
       r->valid_location alone and so passes a null pointer to memcpy. On a
       build with UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer and halt_on_error that aborts the
       worker mid-request, and the client sees a closed connection with no
       response; on an ordinary build the behaviour is undefined.
       The warm now inherits the bit whenever its URI and args are identical
       to the parent request's -- which is the case for every internal warm --
       so it takes the same branch the client request takes. The admin warm
       endpoint is unaffected either way: it supplies its own URI, unrelated
       to the triggering request's, and continues to build the upstream
       request line from that.

    *) Feature: cache_turbo_store_head on|off (default off) makes a HEAD that
       misses the cache fire one internal background subrequest -- a real GET,
       the same mechanism the stale-while-revalidate background refresh uses --
       so a URL that only ever receives HEAD stops being a permanent 100%
       miss. A HEAD may read the cache but can never populate it (nginx sets
       header_only for a HEAD once headers are sent, so the body chain where
       every store in this module lives never runs), which made every uptime
       monitor, link checker and HEAD-issuing crawler pay a full origin round
       trip forever.
       The subrequest flows through the ordinary capture gate and the ordinary
       store path -- no second store path is added -- and is subject to the
       same zone-wide cache_turbo_background_update_max cap as SWR.
       The resulting entry is marked HEAD-derived on the blob and is REFUSED
       to every non-HEAD request at the same serve chokepoint that enforces
       the breaker-only, encoding-class and RFC 9111 3.5 marks: a HEAD-derived
       entry must never answer a GET. Such a GET falls through to the origin
       as on a miss and its own response replaces the entry with an ordinary
       one, so the guard costs at most one extra fetch per URL rather than
       pinning it into permanent misses. Off by default because it spends one
       wasted origin body per HEAD-miss, which only pays for itself on a
       genuinely HEAD-dominated workload.

    *) Feature: the admin warm endpoint's per-request URL cap is now
       configurable via cache_turbo_warm_max N (default 32, matching the
       previous hardcoded ceiling; max 4096), and warming can be driven from
       an external list with POST /_cache?url_file=<path> (one path per
       line) in addition to the existing ?url=<path[,path,...]> query
       string. Both sources fire at most cache_turbo_warm_max subrequests.
       The file read is itself bounded -- max 64 KiB, max 2048 bytes per
       line -- and any violation (missing file, not a regular file, over
       size, an overlong line) is a clean 500 with a JSON error body rather
       than a truncated read or an unbounded allocation.

    *) Bugfix: a dropped auto-Vary variant-index write no longer leaves a
       variant permanently invisible to PURGE. The store path called the L2
       backend's tag_add() and discarded its return value, so an index SADD
       refused before the wire -- an armed connect-backoff window, a failed
       connect, an allocation failure -- silently lost the entry while the
       object itself was cached normally. A later PURGE of the base URI
       enumerates that per-base index set with SMEMBERS, could not see the
       variant, and reported success while the variant kept serving a stale
       representation until its own TTL expired. Nothing logged, nothing
       counted.
       tag_add()/tag_add_many() now report whether the op reached the
       transport (NGX_OK) or was dropped (NGX_ERROR). A drop logs a warning
       and marks the variant's L1 node pending; the next hit on that node
       re-issues the write, and a re-issue that is dropped again leaves the
       node pending so a later hit retries. The store path is unchanged in
       shape: still fire-and-forget, still no blocking Redis round trip, since
       tag_add() returns as soon as the op is handed to or refused by the
       transport. The pending state is one bit inside an already-allocated
       node, so an L2 outage of any length adds no memory: the pending set can
       never exceed the number of resident nodes and is freed with them.
       NGX_OK is a launch acknowledgement, not a server acknowledgement -- a
       write the server rejects after the wire is not covered here.

    *) Performance: ngx_http_cache_turbo_serve() now emits a large cached body
       as a chain of 32 KB ngx_buf_t's that all point into the same refcounted
       shm blob, instead of one buf spanning the whole body; nothing is copied,
       so the serve stays zero-copy, and the existing blob reference is
       acquired and released exactly once per response regardless of chunk
       count. The change bounds how long a single response pins the slab and
       lets a slow reader's writer retire whole bufs instead of holding one
       indivisible allocation. Measured with ci/tools/bench.sh (SIZES=large, 5
       passes): p99 is unchanged -- single-buf and chained runs fall within
       each other's run-to-run spread -- so no latency win is claimed; the
       change is kept for the bounded pinning, not for a measured gain.

    *) Feature: W-TinyLFU admission control, opt-in per zone via
       cache_turbo_zone name=NAME SIZE admission=on. When a NEW key would be
       stored, its estimated access frequency (from the count-min sketch added
       in the previous release) is compared against the probation-tail entry it
       would displace; a candidate that is STRICTLY COLDER is refused and the
       resident entry keeps its slot. Previously eviction was 100% recency and
       a one-hit wonder always displaced a resident entry, with no admission
       decision anywhere in the module.
       OFF BY DEFAULT, and deliberately not flipped the way
       cache_turbo_scan_resistant was: scan resistance only changes WHICH
       resident entry is given up, whereas admission changes whether a response
       is cached at all, and a wrongly refused store is a silent permanent
       extra origin hit. It is a zone parameter rather than a location
       directive because the decision is made inside the shared-memory store
       path, whose L1-vtable signature carries the zone and no location config.
       Fails open in every ambiguous direction: policy off, sketch
       unallocated, empty probation queue, or a tie all ADMIT. Refreshes of an
       already-resident entry are never subject to it (they displace nobody).
       The decision runs strictly BEFORE the eviction loop it would otherwise
       feed, so a refusal removes an eviction rather than driving one -- the
       ordering is pinned structurally by the P4-1b-ADMIT-ORDER canary and the
       termination property by a watchdogged unit test.
       Three new stats on the admin endpoint, in JSON and Prometheus:
       admission_refused / cache_turbo_admission_refused_total,
       sketch_bumps / cache_turbo_sketch_bumps_total (lifetime, survives the
       sketch's aging halvings, so 0 on a live zone means the sketch was never
       allocated) and sketch_gen / cache_turbo_sketch_gen.

    *) Feature: cache_turbo_ignore_set_cookie NAME... relaxes the Set-Cookie
       store floor for explicitly named, non-identifying cookies -- the
       module's equivalent of nginx proxy_ignore_headers Set-Cookie, except
       named rather than blanket. A response carrying any Set-Cookie was
       previously never stored; CMS and analytics stacks that staple _ga, an
       A/B bucket or a consent flag onto otherwise shareable HTML therefore
       made every response uncacheable, which on a WordPress or Magento origin
       with any analytics or consent plugin can be the dominant cause of a
       near-zero hit ratio.
       Fail-closed at every ambiguity: the store is permitted only when EVERY
       Set-Cookie in the response resolves to exactly one RFC 6265 token
       cookie name AND that name is on the configured list. One unlisted name
       among several listed ones, a value with no '=', an empty or quoted
       name, or an embedded space each refuse the store exactly as before.
       The name is parsed from the cookie-pair only, never from the
       attributes, so "Set-Cookie: sessionid=x; Path=/_ga" is not matched by a
       listed _ga.
       Hard-vetoed whenever cookie value-keying is configured in the location
       (any cache_turbo_backend preset active, or any cache_turbo_key_cookie
       set): a request without the key cookie hashes to the anonymous entry
       while the response establishes the segment, so storing it would poison
       the anonymous entry for every anonymous visitor. What is STORED is
       unchanged -- Set-Cookie remains unconditionally stripped before
       serialisation, so no cached entry can ever replay a cookie to a
       different client. Default off; absent the directive behaviour is
       byte-identical to before.

    *) Feature: cache_turbo_background_update_max N adds a zone-wide cap on
       CONCURRENT background-refresh (stale-while-revalidate) subrequests.
       Per-key single-flight already bounded regens for one key, but nothing
       bounded the sum across keys sharing a zone: a synchronized mass
       staleness event (a deploy, a mass purge, a restart touching a shared
       TTL) could fire one background subrequest per stale key at once,
       sending the origin as many concurrent requests as there are stale
       keys while every client is served stale -- invisible to the module's
       own metrics because each key still looks correctly single-flighted.
       Default 0 (unlimited), preserving the previously-ungated behavior
       byte-for-byte until an operator opts in with a cap sized to their
       origin's real concurrent-regen budget. The live in-flight count is
       exposed as bg_inflight in both stats formats (JSON and Prometheus)
       so the cross-key total is now directly observable. Applies to every
       background-refresh subrequest through the shared warm_one() chokepoint
       -- both the SWR dice-winner's own bg-refresh and the admin warm
       endpoint's ?url= warms against the same zone.

    *) Fix: the auto-Vary marker is now written through to L2 (Redis), keyed
       by the same marker hash the L1 marker uses, and consulted on an L1
       marker miss before falling back to a base-key L2 lookup that a varied
       URL's base slot never populates. Previously the shared L2 tier was
       effectively disabled for any Vary-emitting origin with auto_vary on
       (the default) on a multi-node deployment: a node with no L1 marker
       (a fresh boot, an LRU-evicted marker) missed both L1 and L2 for a
       variant a peer had already stored in Redis. The extra L2 GET fires
       only on the marker-cold path; a warm L1 marker still resolves with no
       additional round trip, and a resolved L2 marker self-heals the node's
       own L1 marker so the next request pays nothing.

    *) Feature: cache_turbo_serve_authorized allows a request carrying an
       Authorization header to be SERVED an already-cached entry, where it was
       previously declined before the cache was consulted at all. An API that
       authenticates every call to a shareable public endpoint had a hit ratio
       of exactly zero. The store floor is unchanged and remains ungated: a
       response produced for a credentialed request is still never stored, so
       no private response exists in the cache to expose. Serving is further
       gated per RFC 9111 section 3.5 on the stored response carrying public,
       s-maxage or must-revalidate/proxy-revalidate; entries lacking it --
       including every entry stored before this release -- are refused to a
       credentialed requester and fall through to the origin. Off by default;
       it widens who may read a cached body, so it is an opt-in the operator
       asserts is correct for the endpoint. See README's
       cache_turbo_serve_authorized section.

    *) Feature: cache_turbo_min_uses_window adds time-windowed reset to the
       min_uses miss counter. Without a window, miss_count persists for the
       node's lifetime (reset only on LRU eviction). With a window, the counter
       resets when accessed and `now - last_access > window`, forcing recent
       misses to drive the admission gate instead of counting arbitrary-aged
       accesses. One-hit-wonders that recur in distinct time windows now
       correctly do not satisfy min_uses 2 and bypass the cache unless they
       establish a pattern within the window. Directive accepts 0..86400
       seconds; default 0 (off, preserving v15 lifetime-counter behavior).
       Only affects cache_turbo_min_uses > 1 AND min_uses_window > 0.


Changes with the nginx-cache-turbo-module 1.1.0                 17 Aug 2026

    *) Feature: cache_turbo_vary_ignore <header>... drops named headers from
       a response's Vary line before cache_turbo_auto_vary's whitelist/
       unknown-axis check, treating them as absent rather than safe. Real-
       world Vary axes like Accept (common on APIs/image CDNs), Sec-CH-*
       client hints and Save-Data are not on the built-in safe whitelist, so
       auto_vary alone refuses such a response outright; this directive lets
       an operator opt a specific header out of that refusal without
       widening the whitelist itself. Off by default (nothing ignored); "*",
       Cookie and Authorization are rejected at config time and can never be
       ignored this way. This is a cache-correctness override the operator
       opts into, not a free win -- see README's cache_turbo_vary_ignore
       section.

    *) Behaviour change: cache_turbo_scan_resistant is now ON by default
       (protected_pct=80), where it was previously off. A stock install
       without the directive now gets segmented probation/protected LRU, so
       a crawler, sitemap fetch or scanner walking a large unique keyspace no
       longer evicts the hot set. scanbench.sh measured 0.0% hot-set survival
       under a crawler scan on the old flat-LRU default vs 82.0% with
       segmented LRU at protected_pct=80. This changes eviction ORDER on
       upgrade for any location that did not already set the directive; add
       cache_turbo_scan_resistant off; explicitly to restore the pre-1.1.0
       flat LRU. See README's "Scan-resistant eviction" section.

    *) Security: invalid or out-of-range Redis database suffixes no longer
       echo a credential-bearing DSN into nginx configuration-error logs.

    *) Bugfix: a memcached connection was treated as proven after send()
       accepted the command locally. A peer that closed without replying
       therefore failed to arm connect_backoff and was redialled on every
       miss. The connection is now proven only by the first reply byte.

    *) Bugfix: Redis GET and fire-and-forget reply handlers now require an
       exact RESP boundary before accepting a payload or pooling a keepalive
       connection. Malformed payload delimiters and trailing partial replies
       close the connection instead of desynchronising its next reuse.

    *) Feature: cache_turbo_bypass_stale_uri can retain selected public,
       normally-bypassed responses solely as circuit-breaker fallback. These
       entries remain unreachable as ordinary HIT or STALE responses.

    *) Bugfix: stale-if-error can rescue a response when the origin fails in
       the middle of its body before any bytes have been flushed downstream.
       Live stale-if-error entries also get a second-chance eviction while
       their circuit breaker is open.

    *) Bugfix: over-long cache tags now emit a configuration warning instead
       of being dropped silently, and L2 blob storage no longer reads an
       uninitialised stack field while assembling metadata.

    *) Performance: bounded Cookie classification removes attacker-controlled
       scan work, query-argument classification no longer rescans after 64
       spans, cache-key cookie folding uses one allocation, and the Redis SCAN
       framer resumes linearly across partial reads.

    *) Maintenance: decomposed the configuration, request, restore, classifier,
       Redis and admin paths into smaller helpers without changing their public
       directives; expanded unit, fuzz, preset-parity and runtime regressions
       around the resulting seams.

    *) Bugfix: a ?all=1 L2 all-purge's SCAN walk had only a per-page bound
       (each page's read re-arms the read timeout), never a bound on the
       whole walk. A backend that always returned a non-zero cursor just
       under that timeout could park the purge request for up to the fixed
       page cap's worth of pages -- hours, not seconds. cache_turbo_redis now
       accepts scan_deadline= (default 30s), a wall-clock ceiling checked at
       every page boundary alongside the page cap; 0 disables it (page-cap
       only, prior behaviour). A walk abandoned by the deadline reports the
       purge INCOMPLETE the same way the page cap does, with
       "reason":"deadline" distinguishing it from "reason":"page-cap".

    *) BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: cache_turbo_auto_vary now defaults to on. While it
       defaulted to off, the module ignored the response's own Vary header,
       which left the Vary: Cookie / Authorization veto as dead code: a
       per-user response was stored under a Vary-blind key and served to
       every client, and the stale-serve path widened that window. On by
       default, an un-split Vary axis can no longer serve the wrong
       representation. An origin that emits Vary: Cookie on cacheable public
       pages (Django/Wagtail is the common case) must now set
       cache_turbo_auto_vary off explicitly to keep caching them -- see
       docs/wagtail.md.

    *) Bugfix: a PURGE that reached the Redis variant-index walk leaked the
       client connection. The request was parked with a reference that
       nothing released, because the PRECONTENT phase handler returning
       NGX_DONE never reaches ngx_http_finalize_request. The connection
       stayed open with its fd held until worker shutdown, which logged
       "open socket left in connection". Only reachable with
       cache_turbo_auto_vary on, so the default change above is what exposed
       it.

    *) Adopted the myguard skeleton CI standard: ci/ layout, a single
       pull_request entry point (ci.yml) with every other workflow a
       workflow_call member, the ci/linter/ gate behind one run-all.sh entry
       point, a tracked .githooks/pre-commit, and the badge row and ## CI
       table kept in lockstep by lint-docs-drift.sh.

    *) Fixed ci/tools/lint-shm-lock.sh, which had been reporting "ok" while
       reading ZERO files. Its `cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."` was correct while the
       script lived in tools/ at the repo root and was not updated when the
       tree moved under ci/, so src/*.c matched nothing, the unmatched glob
       stayed a literal, and the `[ -f ]` guard skipped it. The R7 shm-mutex
       invariant is now genuinely checked; it holds in shipped code, so no
       source change was required. An empty selection is exit 2, never 0.

    *) Added lint.yml so the linter gate runs on the PR and not only in the
       commit hook. Both call the same ci/linter/run-all.sh; a CI-only copy
       would drift from the hook and the two would stop agreeing.

    *) Moved Security Scanners beside Fuzzing instead of behind it. The chain
       was sized when scanners took 117s; at 302s it made that lane 970s
       against a 761s budget and the suite's critical path.

    *) Cut the commit hook from 7.4s to 1.47s by no longer running flawfinder,
       cppcheck and semgrep twice per staged commit -- each was both a
       .pre-commit-config.yaml hook and a ci/linter/lint-c.sh scanner, at
       identical thresholds. No gate was dropped: LINT_MODE=all still runs all
       three, and security-scanners.yml owns them on the PR.

    *) Vendored the ast-grep ruleset per module (ci/ast-grep/), so the hook
       gates identically in a standalone clone. ast-grep resolves sgconfig.yml
       by walking UP from the working directory, which outside the superrepo
       finds nothing and scans an empty ruleset while reporting green.

    *) Added gitleaks to ci/linter/install-linters.sh, pinned and
       digest-verified. It gates the staged secret scan and had been present
       only by hand on one build host -- absent from a fresh clone, and absent
       from --check, so nothing reported it missing.

    *) Fixed a Test::Nginx block that asserted only 54 of its 56 requests
       (ci/t/presets/wikijs.t): the --- response_headers array was two entries
       shorter than --- request, and Test::Nginx pairs them positionally, so
       the last pair carried no X-Cache expectation at all.

    *) Gave ci/t/presets/redmine.t TEST 5 and woocommerce.t TEST 6 their own
       paths. cache_turbo_key is $uri, so each shared a cache entry with the
       bypass block above it and its MISS-then-HIT held only because that
       block stores nothing -- an assertion resting on block order.
